#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM5020", unit: "M1")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U1–U2 Cyclic groups",[List the orders of every element of $ZZ slash 8 ZZ$.],[Use the gcd formula.],[For residue $k$, the order is $8/gcd(8,k)$.],[For $k=0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7$, the orders are respectively $1,8,4,8,2,8,4,8$. The formula follows by asking for the least positive $t$ with $8|k t$.],[Explain the formula and treat the zero residue.]),
 p(2,"E","U5 Ring quotients",[Determine whether $ZZ slash 15 ZZ$ is a field and list its units.],[Check coprimality to fifteen.],[The product of three and five is zero.],[It is not a field, since nonzero $[3],[5]$ multiply to zero. Its units are $[1],[2],[4],[7],[8],[11],[13],[14]$, exactly the residues coprime to fifteen by Bézout.],[Justify the full unit list.]),
 p(3,"C","U3–U4 Group structure",[Let $|G|=49$. Prove $G$ is abelian, and show that if some element has order forty-nine then $G$ is cyclic.],[Use the center of a prime-power group.],[The center has order seven or forty-nine.],[The center is nontrivial. If it had order seven, the quotient by the center would be cyclic of order seven, forcing $G$ abelian and contradicting that center size. Thus the center is all of $G$. An element of order forty-nine has a generated subgroup of that size, equal to $G$.],[The cyclicity conclusion is conditional; abelian does not automatically mean cyclic.]),
 p(4,"C","U6–U7 Algebraic extensions",[Let $alpha^3=3$ over $QQ$. Prove the extension degree is three and find $(alpha-1)^(-1)$.],[Use Eisenstein and a geometric-product identity.],[The product with $alpha^2+alpha+1$ is two.],[$x^3-3$ is Eisenstein at three, so it is the minimal polynomial and the degree is three. Since $(alpha-1)(alpha^2+alpha+1)=alpha^3-1=2$, the inverse is $(alpha^2+alpha+1)/2$.],[Prove irreducibility and verify the inverse.]),
 p(5,"A","U3 Universal constructions",[For normal subgroups $N,M$ of $G$, show $g |-> (g N,g M)$ induces an injective homomorphism $G slash (N inter M) -> G slash N times G slash M$. When is it surjective?],[Compute the kernel of the paired map.],[Surjectivity is equivalent to $G=N M$.],[The paired map is a homomorphism with kernel $N inter M$, so its induced map is injective by the first isomorphism theorem. If it is surjective, any pair $(a N,M)$ has a lift $g in M$ with $a N=g N$, so $a in M N=N M$. Conversely if $G=N M$, for prescribed $a N,b M$ write $b^(-1)a=m n$ with $m in M,n in N$. Then $g=b m=a n^(-1)$ has both prescribed cosets.],[Give the kernel and prove both directions of the surjectivity criterion.]),
 p(6,"A","U8 Field structure",[Prove that the fixed field of Frobenius $x |-> x^p$ in $FF_(p^n)$ is exactly $FF_p$.],[Fixed elements are roots of $x^p-x$.],[The prime field already supplies $p$ roots.],[Frobenius fixes the prime field because it fixes one and preserves addition. Thus its fixed set contains $p$ elements. Any fixed element is a root of the degree-$p$ polynomial $x^p-x$, which has at most $p$ roots in a field. Hence there are no others and the fixed field is $FF_p$.],[Show both containment and the cardinality bound.]),
)
